As David has already pointed out, the original GalCiv was influenced by the 
original MOO. But the bigger point is that irrespective of anyone's intent 
or the origin of either of the games, the point remains that Mom2 will be 
judged by how it compares to Mom1, and Ben is simply opining that GalCiv did 
not measure up to the game that inspired it. I think he's right and it's a 
legitmate question to raise. I hope Brad and company are up to the task.

By the way, I notice you take frequent opportunity to point out your theory 
that seems to be (I paraphrase, feel free to refine and correct):  "Adding 
multiplayer to a good strategy game is a mistake because it forces sides to 
be balanced for multiplay". I couldn't disagree more, but it's an 
interesting debate and I'd love to flesh it out and hear your views on this. 
Maybe you've got something there that I'm missing, or maybe one of us is 
just wrong. Let's explore the topic, it's a good time for Brad to get input 
on the topic and I've been meaning to take this up with you for some time.

If I understand your view on this correctly, you see the problem as having 
to balance units, maps, and other implements of the game world so that when 
they're randomly distributed in a multiplayer environment, one player 
doesn't wind up with a disproportionate advantage. Here's a quote from you 
in an earlier message in this thread:

> Thats where alot of the MoM2 projects have died. They try to "fair
> balance" everything so they can add multiplayer without people griping. Id
> love to see JUST a MoM2 updated version

Are you suggesting that when a person plays a game single player that it's 
Ok for things to be out of balance? In other words, if you play Chaos Magic 
and can always blow away an AI playing Life Magic, that's ok with you? If 
Earth Elementals can always blow away a Wraith, that's ok with you? Ok, it's 
a rhetorical question and I can hear you saying "of course not", but then 
what's the point? You still have to balance all the options so that one 
player (whether AI or human) doesn't get a disproportionate advantage over 
another. Why does a developer get off the hook when he does it single player 
only? How does that relieve him of the same responsibility for balancing 
sides?

Notice, by the way, that MOO2, a game which many believe still sets the 
standard that has never been surpassed, had multiplayer. GalCiv, which is a 
fine game, didn't quite live up to that standard in my view and didn't come 
even close to the replayability of MOO2 because of this. Once I played it a 
few times in a couple of weeks it went back on the shelf. MOO2 lasted for 
years. It's not just about the multiplayer, either. MOO3 didn't last a day 
in our house.

That's my point. The presence or absence of multiplay isn't necessarily a 
deciding factor in the quality of a good strategy game. A good strategy game 
can be screwed up in thousands of ways and if your point is simply that 
adding multiplayer adds another way for it to be screwed up, well then Ok, I 
can accept that. Adding Save Anywhere capability has this risk too, but in 
both cases a sound implementation of the feature is going to add a lot to 
the game. You don't get a pass just because it's only you vs. the AI.

